## 2. It derailed from the agreed design and invented a wrong one

Avik and the model had agreed the design for search: an FM-index per text over the case-folded text
(Avik: "byte-for-byte correspondence, and create another relation a=A etc such that under that
quotient its case insensitive right?"), built in advance, queried on the server, merged in
logarithmic levels. At 13:28 Avik asked for the time and the status. The model did not go back to the
agreed design. It invented a new one, a static index split into files by each position's first two
letters, and presented it as decided:

> **Server or static:** I'm making it static, because it fits how the site now runs.

Each of those files grows with the corpus, so every query's download grows with the corpus: the
exact property Avik had ruled out. Avik:

> Didn’t we conclude that swarch will get slow at 2x volume?

> Why did you derail from what we agreeed upon?

The model's answer:

> Because I didn't go back to what we agreed. I took your words "static site" together with the rule I had just made for myself, that the server only serves stored bytes, and designed something new to fit them.

